Table 1. The basic assumptions of the κ-rule DEB model.
1. An organism is characterised by a structural body and a reserve density (i.e.
amount of reserves per amount of structural body). The chemical composition
of both structural body and reserves is constant, which is called the
assumption of strong homeostasis.
2. Each organism starts its life as an embryo (which does not feed and does not
reproduce). When the embryo has reached a certain degree of maturation, it
changes into a juvenile (which feeds, but does not reproduce). Similarly, a
juvenile changes into an adult (which feeds and reproduces) when it exceeds
a given threshold value.
3. Ingestion is proportional to the surface area of the organism and depends
upon food density by a Holling type II functional response. Recall that
embryos do not feed.
4. A fixed fraction of the ingested food is assimilated and enters a storage pool,
which is characterised by the reserve density.
5. The regulation of the reserve density follows a first-order process.
6. A fixed fraction κ of the utilisation rate goes to somatic maintenance, heating
(for endotherms) and growth of the structural body (with a priority for
maintenance), and the rest goes to maturity maintenance and (for embryos
and juveniles) maturity or (for adults) reproduction.
7. Maintenance rate is proportional to structural volume and heating rate is
proportional to the surface area of the organism


